    "Candle Hollow": {
      "name": "Candle Hollow",
      "basicInfo": "A soft-floored hollow in The Dim March where the dim light pools brightest — the region's one named landmark. The moss here is thick as a mattress and utterly silent; even placed stones settle without a sound. A half-collapsed shelf of pale stone overhangs the hollow, and the faint glow seems to rise from the moss itself rather than any source. It is the safest campsite in the march: sound dies here, and nothing that sleeps beneath the moss reaches this far.\n\nLiving Ecology: Pale hush moths gather over the resting light without wingbeat, and the moss forms shallow fox-tail whorls around anything stored here. Dim March decomposers stop cleanly at the hollow's rim, creating a naturally sterile center inside a living preservation biome.",
      "x": 30,
      "y": 25,
      "radius": 2,
      "region": "The Dim March",
      "complexityType": "simple",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "hiddenInfo": "The pooled light is mana in its resting state, made almost solid by the hollow's perfect stillness. Objects left in the hollow change less over time than anywhere else in the Demi-Plane — food stays fresh, steel keeps its edge, enchantments persist. The Chamberlain tested this here before choosing the march as his storage zone. Something older is buried beneath the moss floor at the hollow's center: a sealed mana pocket from the dimension's first days, faintly warm, waiting.\n\nEcological Continuity: The sealed warm pocket beneath the floor is compressed resting mana, not evidence of an older civilization. If opened, it would brighten the Hollow for a season and wake nearby shade-beasts rather than reveal an invented ruin.",
      "factions": [],
      "visited": false,
      "lastVisitedTick": -1,
      "visitedAreas": []
    },
    "The Grey Fog Wall": {
      "name": "The Grey Fog Wall",
      "basicInfo": "The southern edge of The Grey Boundary — a wall of grey fog stretching horizon to horizon. Cool, damp, opaque. Walking into it feels like walking into nothing: no resistance, no ground change, just a growing sense that forward is not a direction that exists here. Shapes flicker too briefly to name within the fog, and sound carries a half-beat too late. This is the firmest edge of the dimension — it does not expand here, and the fog does not retreat.\n\nLiving Ecology: Boundary moss and wine-red foxfire trace the Wall's base, making spatial strain visible as ripples through heather before the fog itself changes. Grey moths travel parallel to the edge and never enter it.",
      "x": 20,
      "y": 10,
      "radius": 2,
      "region": "The Grey Boundary",
      "complexityType": "simple",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "hiddenInfo": "The fog is fixed because it anchors the Demi-Plane to a stable point in the dimensional void. If it moved, the dimension's position relative to the mortal world would drift and the Demi-Plane Gate could become unpredictable. The shades within the fog are not hostile — they are half-formed echoes of places the dimension has considered being. Rarely, something genuinely other slips along the wall and is quietly stopped by it.\n\nEcological Continuity: Flickering shapes in the fog are boundary refractions, drifting fauna, or concrete intrusions with an actual path—not automatic people, ruins, or messages from nowhere. The Wall reveals no facts merely because someone watches it.",
      "factions": [],
      "visited": false,
      "lastVisitedTick": -1,
      "visitedAreas": []
    },
    "Anchor Cairn": {
      "name": "Anchor Cairn",
      "basicInfo": "A triple-stacked cairn of pale anchor-stone on the moor — one of several boundary markers the Chamberlain has placed along the southern edge. Each stone is cool, heavy for its size, and utterly still: no moss grows on it, no insect lands on it, no fog condenses on it. A small shimenawa rope ties the top stone. From here the fog wall can be watched safely.\n\nLiving Ecology: Anchor beetles pack grey grit beneath the stacked stones, and their closed burrow rings quietly extend the cairn's stabilizing influence through the surrounding moor. A single wine-red flame rests in its lee on windless nights.",
      "x": 50,
      "y": 35,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "The Grey Boundary",
      "complexityType": "simple",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "hiddenInfo": "Anchor-stone is condensed dimensional fixedness — literalized stability, which is why it stays unmarked by moss, insects, or fog. An older cairn stands nearby that predates the Chamberlain: its stones are arranged in a pattern matching no tradition he knows, and it hums faintly when the fog wall ripples. He has never claimed it and suspects it was not built by hands at all.\n\nEcological Continuity: The cairn is an observation point within the existing southern anchor system, not a second Anchor item or alternate entrance. Moving a loose surface stone changes a reading; it does not grant control over the plane.",
