    "Moonwhite Strand": {
      "name": "Moonwhite Strand",
      "basicInfo": "The long dune line behind the Stillwater Shore — moon-white sand dunes that never shift, backed by pale-flowered salt-grass whose hollow stalks chime once when nothing else disturbs the air. The strand has the dimension's softest footing and the clearest silence; walking it feels like being the first person ever to cross it. Dune foxes den along its folds, and the sand records every track for days without filling them.\n\nLiving Ecology: Moonplovers nest among pale-flowered dune-grass, and tide moths suspend pearlescent silk between saltgrass stems. Cedar needles from the Sanctuary occasionally arrive without wind and become the center of new dune gardens.",
      "x": 35,
      "y": 45,
      "radius": 3,
      "region": "The Quiet Shore",
      "complexityType": "simple",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "hiddenInfo": "The dunes never shift because the strand is a record, not a landscape — the sand holds whatever is written in it, including footprints, small offerings, and half-covered things. Shell crystal forms inside the oldest salt-grass stems. Rarely, an impossible reflection appears in the sand's hollows at dusk: a face, a boat, a figure — usually washed away by morning. Tideline caves open at the strand's western edge on quiet days, tunnels into the duneface that go back several body-lengths and drain into the not-water without flooding.\n\nEcological Continuity: Pale driftwood is condensed from compatible organic patterns carried by the boundary, then accepted into the Shore's ecology. It does not imply a sea, shipwreck, or outside settlement unless a concrete event establishes one.",
