    "Demi-Plane Gate": {
      "name": "Demi-Plane Gate",
      "basicInfo": "The standing arch within the Gateway Chamber's pale stone circle — the door by which the creator arrives. When Tsukiko uses Demi-Plane Gate, she steps through here. When the Demi-Plane Anchor opens a portal, it opens beneath the arch. No other entrance to the dimension exists without her permission; the gate is the Demi-Plane's single intentional threshold, and the garden spirits keep its approach swept and flowered without ever being asked. On either side, the gate presents as a standing torii with paired lanterns — readable as a shrine entrance to anyone with fox blood, indistinguishable from roadside stonework to anyone without.\n\nLiving Ecology: Petals and seed-dust approaching the arch settle into two crescent beds rather than crossing accidentally. When the Gate opens, its lanterns flare in the colors of the destination's weather, and the garden falls still for one breath as the realm tests what has entered.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 0,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Heartgate Basin",
      "complexityType": "simple",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "hiddenInfo": "The arch is not furniture but a fixed point in two worlds at once — its foundations reach into both the Demi-Plane and whatever side the creator stands on when she opens the way. This is why the basin's name is Heartgate: everything heartward begins here. The Gate keeps itself locked; not even the Chamberlain can open it for anyone but the creator. When the anchor point in the Last Edge is stable, the arch hums faintly — a tone the Chamberlain checks by ear like a landlord listening to pipes.\n\nEcological Continuity: The threshold distinguishes permission from ecology: authorized travelers pass, but unfamiliar spores, parasites, curses, and loose enchantment residue are held in a ring of pale moss until the Basin accepts, neutralizes, or rejects them. This is a natural safeguard, not an omniscient detector of identity or intent.",
