    "Golden Upland": {
      "name": "Golden Upland",
      "basicInfo": "The broad sunlit plain of The Easthold — harder ground, golden dry-grass flats, and the richest light in the dimension, warm without a source. Dust motes hang in the air like slow sparks of gold, unmoving. The upland feels like the space where the dimension is pushing outward against its own edge: stretching, straining, trying to grow beyond what has been thought of so far.\n\nLiving Ecology: Vent moths rise from ember shrubs at dusk, sunscale lizards carry heat into their burrows, and distant hawks bend the gold air around their wings. Cold Mirror Mere wind occasionally sends a moving violet stripe across the northern grass.",
      "x": 15,
      "y": 40,
      "radius": 4,
      "region": "The Easthold",
      "complexityType": "wilderness",
      "detailType": "basic",
      "known": true,
      "hiddenInfo": "The upland's constant warmth draws phenomena: sun hawks nest in thermal columns of raw mana, mana vents breathe heat and light from stone seams, and hidden workshops have appeared where tools were once imagined strongly — half-formed benches, anvil outlines, racks of unidentifiable instruments that dissolve if studied too directly. Temporary summoning phenomena interrupt the flat ground erratically: half-built circles, calls half-heard. Golden lizards eat mana-stone dust and their shed scales concentrate it — a shortcut prospectors have not yet thought of.\n\nEcological Continuity: Half-formed workshop shapes are harmless expressions of remembered utility and fade unless development gives them material, labor, and purpose. They contain no free finished equipment or unexplained craftspeople.",
