    "Shinboku": {
      "name": "Shinboku",
      "basicInfo": "The sacred heart-tree of the Veiled Fox Sanctuary — a vast, still shinboku standing at the sanctuary's center, ringed with a rope of braided gold and white paper in the oldest kami tradition. Its canopy is always faintly lit from within, its roots vanish into moss that lights underfoot, and the air beneath it tastes of cedar smoke and sugared ginger. Stone foxes face outward from its circle; nothing uninvited crosses it.\n\nLiving Ecology: At each false dawn the canopy releases nine slow-turning leaves of light. They dissolve before touching the moss and send a visible pulse through the sanctuary's fungi, spring, lantern trees, and western rootways.",
      "x": 50,
      "y": 40,
      "radius": 2,
      "region": "Veiled Fox Sanctuary",
      "complexityType": "simple",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "hiddenInfo": "The shinboku feeds on Tsukiko's presence — aether drawn from her divinity seeps through its roots and pools beneath the estate as a reserve only she can call upon. The tree is simultaneously shrine, battery, and covenant; the Chamberlain has proposed three times that the Demi-Plane's mana infrastructure route through it, and been politely declined each time. Shrine guardians walk its ring at set hours, and the sacred birds roost only in its lowest branches. Beneath the roots, small hidden shrine niches hold offerings left by the sanctuary's spirit-folk — unrecorded, undisturbed, and unread by anyone living.\n\nEcological Continuity: The tree's stored divine reserve belongs to Tsukiko alone. Ordinary ecological circulation uses only the softened overflow processed by fungus and root; wildlife feeding beneath the canopy gains health and luminescence, never her memories, authority, divinity, or secret knowledge.",
