    "Torii Procession": {
      "name": "Torii Procession",
      "basicInfo": "The vermilion gate-line of the Veiled Fox Sanctuary — a procession of torii gates rising from the ferned woodland in sequence, each frame framing the next, stone paths crossing beneath them in shimenawa-bound silence. Passing each gate feels like setting down a small burden. The procession winds toward the sanctuary's deeper ground and, eventually, toward the Hidden Estate — for those who are bidden.\n\nLiving Ecology: Violetwing moths roost beneath the lintels and brighten one gate ahead of an authorized traveler. Moss paths weave around root nurseries, while falling red leaves pause beneath each torii before choosing a side.",
      "x": 20,
      "y": 20,
      "radius": 3,
      "region": "Veiled Fox Sanctuary",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "hiddenInfo": "The gates are not decoration but a threshold ceremony in permanent form — each torii is a ward that reads intent more precisely than the last. Uninvited walkers find the procession could lengthen without moving: no gate becomes the final gate, and the woods politely return them to the beginning. Permitted visitors feel the gates brighten in recognition. Occasionally a gate in the line appears rearranged from the previous day — the spirit-folk maintain the procession as a living ritual, and the sequence of gates has never once repeated itself exactly.\n\nEcological Continuity: The changing sequence is partly ecological maintenance: gates move a few paces to protect new dens, mushrooms, and root channels while preserving the same destination and ward function. No new settlement or hidden populace is created by those changes.",
