    "Mana-Flow Paths": {
      "name": "Mana-Flow Paths",
      "basicInfo": "The living trails of The Threshold March — paths that align with the Demi-Plane's internal mana circulation, running from the Gateway Clearing's edge to the corridor's northern mouth. Walking them feels subtly restorative, as if the dimension is grateful for the attention; ambient mana recharges slightly with every stride. The trails never quite stay where they were mapped — they breathe and shift with the dimension's circulation like veins rather than roads.\n\nLiving Ecology: Path beetles loosen compressed soil while pale foxlets patrol for the grubs that would strip the root sheath. The visible glow braids, straightens, or knots with the Mana Heart's circulation.",
      "x": 20,
      "y": 25,
      "radius": 3,
      "region": "The Threshold March",
      "complexityType": "wilderness",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "hiddenInfo": "The paths are the dimension's circulation made walkable — they trace mana channels from the Mana Heart and shift as the flow itself shifts, which is why no map of them holds for long. Mana constructs occasionally walk these routes in purposeful silence: brief sentry-forms the dimension spawns to patrol its own circulation, dissolving once the route is checked. Walking the full corridor in a single session grants a mild sense of alignment and slightly clearer intent for the rest of the day. The Chamberlain tracks path-shifts as a health metric — straight means steady, tangled means strained circulation.\n\nEcological Continuity: Following a complete route exposes a traveler to gentle ambient flow but grants no invented skill, level, or lasting bonus. Any restoration remains limited to what established mechanics support.",
      "factions": [],
      "visited": false,
      "lastVisitedTick": -1,
      "visitedAreas": []
    },
    "Mineral Springs": {
      "name": "Mineral Springs",
      "basicInfo": "The flowing stone-veins of The Stone Rise — clear mineral springs rising from the grey rock in cold, faintly metallic threads, pooling in terrace basins the moss-lichen has held for seasons. The water tastes of iron and clean cold, and drinking it settles the breath. Clay banks around the richest springs hold spirit minerals that glitter when the light is right.\n\nLiving Ecology: Translucent spring-snails browse crystal moss along the basins and leave empty pearl shells where the water turns. Moss-horn goats carry lichen spores between the upper and lower terraces.",
      "x": 30,
      "y": 30,
      "radius": 3,
      "region": "The Stone Rise",
      "complexityType": "wilderness",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "hiddenInfo": "The springs rise along the dimension's structural grain — the water is drawn through Foundation Ridge's bedrock and emerges carrying trace spirit mineral, which is why it never stagnates. Mineral elementals surface at the deepest pools briefly and are harmless if ignored: they reform into the water if left undisturbed. Small hidden cave mouths thread the banks behind the springs — some stretch back into the hillside, some hold exposed ore seams, and at least one runs with porous rock that slowly filters mana-water. The Chamberlain has sampled all three springs monthly; their mineral content rises imperceptibly, a fact he logs as encouraging growth.\n\nEcological Continuity: Mineral elementals here are non-sapient expressions of water meeting dense spirit ore. Ignoring them leaves the springs undisturbed; observing one reveals no buried history or mineral map beyond what is physically visible.",
      "factions": [],
      "visited": false,
      "lastVisitedTick": -1,
      "visitedAreas": []
    },
    "High Outlook": {
      "name": "High Outlook",
      "basicInfo": "The great outcropping of The Stone Rise — a bare grey headland that, on clear days, sees the entire dimension from the Quiet Shore's pale coast to the Veiled Edge's shimmering curtain. On clouded days it still sees farther than anything else in the Demi-Plane, and the wind there carries the faint suggestion of every region at once. Mountain goats control the approach; cliff drakes nest below its southern face.\n\nLiving Ecology: Crystal-wing swifts circle below the crown while prairie seed caught on cliff-drake scales sprouts in cracks. On clear days, the connected water, root, wind, and migration corridors can be followed across all twenty-five tiles.",
      "x": 15,
      "y": 45,
      "radius": 2,
      "region": "The Stone Rise",
      "complexityType": "simple",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "hiddenInfo": "The Outlook is the dimension's highest observation post — standing here, exactly oriented, trips an intentional quiet: the hum of the grass, the glow of the rift, the tint of the torii, all faintly audible at once, like a map made of sound. The Chamberlain climbs here before major reports. A natural cave behind the Outlook's crown was hollowed out by the dimension itself, dry and floor-smooth, apparently reserved — for what, even he does not guess. The cliff drakes below are territorial but know his hood.\n\nEcological Continuity: The sound-map conveys real, present ecological signals only. It cannot identify hidden actors, private conversations, motives, or events outside sensory reach.",
      "factions": [],
      "visited": false,
      "lastVisitedTick": -1,
      "visitedAreas": []
    },
    "Foundation Ridge": {
      "name": "Foundation Ridge",
      "basicInfo": "The long grey ridgeline anchoring The Stone Rise — bedrock exposed at the spine, older-feeling than anything else in the young dimension. The moss here grows in precise bands, the air is stillest at its base, and sound behaves oddly near it: echoes fall a fraction early rather than late. It looks like any other ridge; it feels like keel timber.\n\nLiving Ecology: Bands of emerald, cobalt, and silver lichen make the bedrock's condition visible, while stone-boring beetles open tiny safe channels for springwater. The guardian's patrol leaves no damage or trail beyond a momentary hush.",
      "x": 25,
      "y": 15,
      "radius": 3,
      "region": "The Stone Rise",
      "complexityType": "wilderness",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "hiddenInfo": "The Ridge is the dimension's structural foundation — the bedrock on which the Demi-Plane is extrapolated from the Last Edge's anchor. Destabilizing it would set the whole dimension adrift; the Chamberlain does not know this. The Mana Heart's guardian entity patrols the ridge's perimeter when not at its post. Narrow passes thread over and beneath the ridge — some natural, some carved by no visible hand — and the rock is the dimension's densest: iron-rich, spirit-mineral-bearing, and slow to crack. Mining here should be done with reverence rather than appetite.\n\nEcological Continuity: Emerald indicates balanced flow, cobalt high pressure, and silver structural strain. These are observable conditions, not a replacement for the Mana Heart, Last Edge anchor, or development mechanics.",
      "factions": [],
      "visited": false,
      "lastVisitedTick": -1,
      "visitedAreas": []
    },
    "Twilight Pools": {
      "name": "Twilight Pools",
      "basicInfo": "The heart of The Mirror Mere — a spread of still pools under a sky that is forever twilight here, connected by gentle channels and ringed in reed-beds. The water faintly glows and holds reflection as if it remembers what stood before it; fish-sized lights drift beneath the surface, never quite taking shape. Everything in this place is reflected: the pools show what stands above them — and occasionally, what does not.\n\nLiving Ecology: Moonfin school beneath violet reeds, glass frogs pulse softly from lily pads, and opaline grit from the Veiled Edge turns in slow galaxies along the bottom. Ripples travel in pairs: the movement and its remembered echo.",
      "x": 25,
      "y": 35,
      "radius": 4,
      "region": "The Mirror Mere",
      "complexityType": "wilderness",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "hiddenInfo": "The pools regulate the dimension's mana circulation — absorbing excess during peaks, releasing during lulls; surface stillness is the Chamberlain's literal health check. But the water also remembers. Reflections sometimes outlast their originals — a bird flies off, its reflection stays. Focused gazing can trace these lingering images to hidden islands that surface in the shallows, or to temporary spirit paths that open across the channels at dusk, walkable until the reflection that anchored them fades. Mirror fish feed on the strongest memories in the water and their pearls are born from what is most dearly held.\n\nEcological Continuity: A pool can repeat a reflected movement or weather pattern after it passes, but it supplies no identity, motive, history, or dialogue that was never physically reflected.",
      "factions": [],
      "visited": false,
      "lastVisitedTick": -1,
      "visitedAreas": []
    },
    "Reflection Basin": {
      "name": "Reflection Basin",
      "basicInfo": "The Mirror Mere's largest single mirror — a broad, glassy expanse of water so still it doubles the twilight sky without a single tremor. Luminous reeds ring its edges; mirror-lotus bloom in those places where the water shows a thing and its reflection in perfect agreement. Visitors find their own reflections here clearer than reality: posture straightened, expression honest, tiredness falling away from the image if not the body.\n\nLiving Ecology: The Mere's largest unbroken surface holds the whole twilight sky and releases stored light through mirror-lotus roots at false dawn. Reflection herons patrol its shallows without disturbing more than one ring at a time.",
      "x": 15,
      "y": 30,
      "radius": 3,
      "region": "The Mirror Mere",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "hiddenInfo": "The Basin is the reservoir's deepest point and the circulation's master regulator — what shows still here means the whole dimension is stable. Submerged ruins lie beneath it, visible through the clear depth on quiet days: pale stonework that no one built, arranged as if some older idea of a temple preceded the dimension and the Mere remembered it. Visions stand whole in the still water — not predictions, but honest inspections: whoever looks long enough sees themselves as the dimension sees them, which is a kinder reading than most receive. Water spirits maintain the ruin's visibility by polishing the water from below, for reasons they keep politely to themselves.\n\nEcological Continuity: Visions require a concrete source—stored reflection, active divination, established magic, or actual observer. The basin never becomes a convenient omniscient screen.",
      "factions": [],
      "visited": false,
      "lastVisitedTick": -1,
      "visitedAreas": []
    },
    "Frontier Fade": {
      "name": "Frontier Fade",
      "basicInfo": "The misted rim of The Still Frontier — the line where willed ground becomes unwilled. Ground grows indistinct here, colors wash toward grey, and sound carries strangely; walk far enough and the world simply stops, not with a wall but with a fading. Snow travels into the Fade and stops moving. The constant, faint sensation is that standing here is standing at the edge of someone's attention.\n\nLiving Ecology: Frost-tail fox tracks become constellations at the rim before fading, and unmoving snow hangs over root nurseries waiting for the next safe outward growth. The last conifers lean inward rather than toward nonexistent wind.",
      "x": 25,
      "y": 25,
      "radius": 3,
      "region": "The Still Frontier",
      "complexityType": "wilderness",
      "detailType": "basic",
      "known": true,
      "hiddenInfo": "The Fade advances as the Demi-Plane's ambient potential seeps outward and retreats when its mana runs low — the Chamberlain reads its position as the dimension's health report. The fade-line is unstable within Reach's own limits: objects left in it change; travelers who linger too long report their footprints fading before them, in the direction ahead rather than behind. Frozen shrines stand inside the Fade's reach — iced mid-ceremony, abandoned by no one known — and strange weather pockets hold their ground in its mists: one small snow that has never fallen is always just beginning here. The Fade is where expansion will begin when the creator chooses; the frontier always leaves a little room at the rim first, as if thinking ahead on its own.\n\nEcological Continuity: Frozen shrines and ceremonial-looking ice forms are natural crystallizations of Tsukiko's realm-memory unless play establishes a maker. They do not add lost inhabitants to the young plane.",
      "factions": [],
      "visited": false,
      "lastVisitedTick": -1,
      "visitedAreas": []
    },
    "Frostpine Ridge": {
      "name": "Frostpine Ridge",
      "basicInfo": "The high spine of The Still Frontier — a ridgeline of pale conifers with frost-glinting needles beneath a paler sky, under weather that is not quite weather: silver breath-mist, snow falling in slow spirals, frost-formed foxfire burning statically in the branches. Crest views give back the whole northern dimension; the furred sound is layered winter hush. Pale wolves and frost foxes pace here, watching travelers with an owner's calm rather than hunger.\n\nLiving Ecology: Glass-antler elk open migration lanes between pale trunks, spiral owls ride upward snow, and blue lace-fungus turns fallen frostwood into warm soil beneath the ice.",
      "x": 15,
      "y": 35,
      "radius": 4,
      "region": "The Still Frontier",
      "complexityType": "wilderness",
      "detailType": "basic",
      "known": true,
      "hiddenInfo": "The ridge is the frontier's oldest ground: its pale conifers were the dimension's first attempt to create weather and have not stopped since. Cave mouths thread beneath the root systems — cold hollows lined with frozen foxfire, some stretching back under the ridge into pockets of unmelted ice that hold mana like clear stone. Snow wraiths roost along the crest in settled weather; their paths mark the safest route across the scree. The Chamberlain visits twice a year and leaves a small frozen foxfire offering at a shrine none of his records mention building.\n\nEcological Continuity: The unrecorded-looking shrine is a foxfire-and-root formation produced by the region's memory of reverence. The Chamberlain's offerings acknowledge the place; they do not prove another population built it.",
      "factions": [],
      "visited": false,
      "lastVisitedTick": -1,
      "visitedAreas": []
    },
    "Ghost Valleys": {
      "name": "Ghost Valleys",
      "basicInfo": "The shallow valleys of The Pale Reaches, filled with glowing silver fog that never fully settles or fully lifts. The valley floors shift texture without changing shape, and the fog gives back shapes: lanes, rooftops, lights, faces — a place seems to have been here before, then was not. Walking one valley end to end is never quite the same walk twice.\n\nLiving Ecology: Fogmice and pearl-foot hares leave temporary tracks across miniature landscapes that complete a season before noon. Veil skates graze silver spores inches above the valley floor.",
      "x": 25,
      "y": 30,
      "radius": 4,
      "region": "The Pale Reaches",
      "complexityType": "wilderness",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "hiddenInfo": "The valley mist is raw dimensional potential in semi-tangible form — the Reaches' heart of the unspent. Uncontrolled, it forms fleeting ghost-landscapes that dissolve in seconds, sometimes matching places the creator has seen or dreamed; it has twice produced a doorway street that appears on no floor plan anywhere. Fog elementals condense from the densest mist: patient, watchful, and dissolving peacefully when entertained or ignored with equal confidence. Pale stone beasts stand among the benches like statuary until approached, then walk away precisely as if they had somewhere to be. The valleys have no memory of anyone's visit: footprints cool and fade within the hour.\n\nEcological Continuity: Focused intention shapes raw mist, while uncontrolled formations remain filtered by lichen and wildlife into harmless temporary ecology. They reveal remembered forms, not secret truth or prophecy.",
      "factions": [],
      "visited": false,
      "lastVisitedTick": -1,
      "visitedAreas": []
    },
    "Silver-Lichen Flats": {
      "name": "Silver-Lichen Flats",
      "basicInfo": "The broad benches of The Pale Reaches — white-grey stone terraces washed in silver lichen that lights faintly like constellations underfoot. By day the flats look austere; by night they glow in slow constellations that reform between one glance and the next. Pale crystals collect in the bench hollows where the fog pools overnight, and the whole flat has the settled patience of a place prepared in advance for something that has not yet been decided.\n\nLiving Ecology: Constellation lichen brightens beneath stoneback grazers and dims after releasing spores into the dawn fog. Each broad bench resembles a night sky laid level underfoot.",
      "x": 15,
      "y": 20,
      "radius": 3,
      "region": "The Pale Reaches",
      "complexityType": "simple",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "hiddenInfo": "The lichen's constellations are not random — they form the same arrangements of light twice a season, patterns the Chamberlain has charted and cannot decode. He believes they are the potential deciding what it will be, written faintly ahead of time. Silver lichen is the region's most valuable resource: a natural mana-conductive coating for warded surfaces that no artificer has learned to imitate. Temporary creatures made of potential occasionally cross the flats in perfect daylight, and travelers' brief structures — a camp, a bridge, a well — sometimes surface in the bench hollows after fog-heavy nights, half-complete, the potential rehearsing invention.\n\nEcological Continuity: Lichen patterns map local moisture and potential density rather than fate. Reading them can guide harvest and travel, never grant automatic knowledge of distant events.",
      "factions": [],
      "visited": false,
      "lastVisitedTick": -1,
      "visitedAreas": []
    },
    "Shining Channels": {
      "name": "Shining Channels",
      "basicInfo": "The alive watercourses of The North Field — slow, glittering channels that cross the plain, appear overnight, and vanish again when their work is done. reed elk wade the shallows, channel serpents glide beneath with curious eyes, and the water catches the ribbon-cloud skies like slow wire. The channels 'stay' where the dimension needs circulation most, which makes them read as an unfinished canal system designed by a patient architect with a sense of humor.\n\nLiving Ecology: Ribbonfin shoals follow water that sometimes climbs reeds into the sky, while lantern geese seed the next channel from their luminous plumage. Abandoned beds become blue-flowered silt gardens within days.",
      "x": 25,
      "y": 30,
      "radius": 4,
      "region": "The North Field",
      "complexityType": "wilderness",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "hiddenInfo": "The channels are the north wind's visible hand — surface mana circulation expressed as water. They appear where the spreading dimension needs carrying and vanish when the carrying is done, so no map has ever held them for a full season. Reed elk follow the channels with uncanny accuracy, appearing to know where the water will be next; following the elk is the only reliable navigation. Spirit crossings form where two channels touch — shallow bars of hardened water where river spirits allow foot traffic for a night's span. At least one channel bed holds a cooled, wide, flattened vein of mana-rich silt that glows faintly at dusk — a hidden pond no visitor marked, which the serpents guard gently and the Chamberlain does not know exists.\n\nEcological Continuity: The channels relocate water through connected underground flow; they do not vanish it or create limitless supply. Blocking one redirects consequences into adjacent regions.",
      "factions": [],
      "visited": false,
      "lastVisitedTick": -1,
      "visitedAreas": []
    },
    "Northwind Plain": {
      "name": "Northwind Plain",
      "basicInfo": "The broad heart of The North Field—windswept river-grass beneath ribbon-cloud skies where a faint scentless wind blows from the north. Rain falls upward in glittering curtains on still afternoons, temporary channels silver the low ground, and reed elk move between firm grass islands. The terrain could support farmland, a great hall, or an arena through established development, but it is already a complete boreal plain rather than land waiting for inhabitants.\n\nLiving Ecology: Reed elk move through wind-laid grass in wide arcs, rain-swifts swim upward through glittering curtains, and every cloud has a dim twin traveling beneath the shallow soil.",
      "x": 15,
      "y": 25,
      "radius": 4,
      "region": "The North Field",
      "complexityType": "wilderness",
      "detailType": "basic",
      "known": true,
      "hiddenInfo": "The north wind is the dimension breathing; the Chamberlain checks its steadiness here before every health report. Temporary channels surface overnight, wetland birds reorganize their nests each season, and short-lived tree islands grow where the wind bends, leaving fertile soil when their cycle ends. Construction takes root readily because water, silt, and seed already circulate here—not because the plain creates inhabitants or completes projects unaided.\n\nEcological Continuity: A stopped north wind signals circulation strain but not its cause. Diagnosis still requires evidence from the Mana Heart, paths, water, or direct investigation.",
      "factions": [],
      "visited": false,
      "lastVisitedTick": -1,
      "visitedAreas": []
    },
    "Folding Ridges": {
      "name": "Folding Ridges",
      "basicInfo": "The paper-fold uplands of The Fold — ridges that curve like folded paper, valleys that loop back on their own paths, grass and heath and low stone rising and repeating in wave-terraces. Disorienting but not dangerous: the region is an unfinished experiment with elevation, walked like a letter being written and rewritten. Standing on one crest, the same crest is often visible twice more, at distances that disagree.\n\nLiving Ecology: Copper foxfire runs along each crease at dusk, revealing wildlife routes invisible in daylight. Fold-martens move nuts between distant slopes, and twin-root shrubs flower on both sides at once.",
      "x": 25,
      "y": 30,
      "radius": 4,
      "region": "The Fold",
      "complexityType": "wilderness",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "hiddenInfo": "The warped geometry is a natural defense — intruders entering without permission become lost as folding paths turn them around without their realizing it. The ridges also hide as much as they defend: hidden pockets of folded space hold airless dry caves, impossible stone chambers with no entrances visible from any direction, and shortcuts that connect ridges the long way around by not being long at all. Spatial wisps drift out of creases where the ground is thinnest; capturing one's light rewards the finder with a brief glimpse of the Fold from above, as a folded map viewed flat. Nothing here is a permanent marker because nothing here holds still for one. The Chamberlain keeps his survey of the ridges sealed and knot-string-based, refusing to trust any distance he measured only once.\n\nEcological Continuity: A captured spatial wisp can show the Fold's current physical arrangement briefly, not creatures hidden from view, private activity, or future configurations.",
      "factions": [],
      "visited": false,
      "lastVisitedTick": -1,
      "visitedAreas": []
    },
    "Returning Valley": {
      "name": "Returning Valley",
      "basicInfo": "The loop-back valley at the heart of The Fold — a wave-terrace floor whose paths consistently deliver walkers back where they began, no matter which direction they entrust their pace to. Grass grows in neat pleats along the terraces, and fold sprites sit in the creases like linen passengers. Travelers report that leaving takes exactly the effort entering did: the valley is not a trap but an introduction to how the Fold thinks.\n\nLiving Ecology: Pleatback tortoises graze the valley floor while origami-wing cicadas pulse from the shaded folds. Two patches of twin-site herb bloom in perfect rhythm despite standing on apparently separate terraces.",
      "x": 15,
      "y": 45,
      "radius": 3,
      "region": "The Fold",
      "complexityType": "simple",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "hiddenInfo": "The Valley returns everyone because it is the dimension's practicing ground for hospitality — safe, patient, and gently circular. Successful explorers pass through it by walking with intention rather than speed: those who leave in a chosen direction rather than away from something exit cleanly the first time. Strange herbs grow only in the valley's pleated shade and cross-pollinate with themselves between two sites at once. One hidden cave mouth opens on the valley's north wall for a single hour on settled afternoons, breathing warm air from elsewhere in the dimension: a shortcut that has never twice led to the same place, and by consensus should stay unmarked.\n\nEcological Continuity: Leaving by chosen direction works because the geometry responds to stable intent, not because it judges courage or reads private thoughts. The route cannot force or infer a character's decision.",
      "factions": [],
      "visited": false,
      "lastVisitedTick": -1,
      "visitedAreas": []
    },
    "The Shimmering Curtain": {
      "x": 40,
      "y": 20,
      "radius": 3,
      "region": "The Veiled Edge",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "name": "The Shimmering Curtain",
      "basicInfo": "The veil's most luminous reach — a sweeping arc where the boundary stands closest and glows brightest, rippling slowly like light through deep water. The spirit-grass ends here in a clean line, and beyond it the shimmering curtain rises from the ground to no visible height. Ground-aura light pools at its base in slow-moving bands. Sound softens within a few strides of it. Touching it is cool and faintly yielding, like pressing a hand against the surface of a still lake from underneath.\n\nLiving Ecology: Veil moths rest against the upright light like leaves on glass, and shoals of foxfire minnows swim through the dense aurora pooled at its base. Touch sends color through nearby grass roots without opening a passage.",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Curtain thins where intent is focused upon it. Sustained, deliberate attention opens layers of translucency through which the space between dimensions becomes briefly visible — distant realities as faint constellations, mortal leylines as drifting ribbons of gold. The Chamberlain does not know this has been observed.\n\nEcological Continuity: Sustained intent can reveal layers beyond the curtain under the established boundary behavior, but looking grants no access, automatic recognition, or factual knowledge of distant realms."
    },
    "Veilwatch Point": {
      "x": 20,
      "y": 40,
      "radius": 2,
      "region": "The Veiled Edge",
      "complexityType": "wilderness",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "name": "Veilwatch Point",
      "basicInfo": "A low stone rise at the edge's clearing — bare grey rock in an otherwise silver-grassed plain, offering the clearest, safest view of the shimmering boundary. From its summit the whole arc of the veil is visible, and the aurora ground-glow reads as slow weather over the meridian. Aurora wisps gather above the point at dusk, and the light they shed is steady enough to read by. A natural watch-post and the region's favored resting place.\n\nLiving Ecology: Aurora crystal grows only in the rise's northeast shadow, where prism beetles graze excess light and boundary walkers rest without leaving tracks. Every pulse from the Curtain crosses the stone as visible color.",
      "hiddenInfo": "The bedrock of Veilwatch Point is the last truly solid stone before the boundary — it does not hum, glow, or shift, and its stillness acts as an anchor. Boundary spirits are drawn to rest here; aurora crystal forms in the rock's shadow on the northeast face.\n\nEcological Continuity: The point is a reliable observation station because it is stable, not because it sees everything. Its signs must be witnessed and interpreted, and can still be misunderstood."
    },
