      "description": "Inscribe an ofuda — a paper talisman carrying ritual intent — that stays where it is placed until its purpose is met or its ink fades. An ofuda's strength, duration, and number scale only with the inscriber's actual rank, mana, ritual skill, and kami-standing; there is no fixed ceiling. A novice's warding strip mends one weak ward-line for a night. A true Miko's ofuda can seal a door against demons, still a restless grave, hallow a harvest, guard a companion in sleep, or anchor a barrier that outlasts the battle that would have broken a lesser ward. Each ofuda carries one purpose chosen at inscription: warding, sealing, blessing, holding, mending, or warning. It remains dormant until its condition is met — an intruder crosses the threshold, a spirit nears, an injury touches flesh — and then it acts with exactly the force its maker instilled. The paper is perishable: rain, fire, deliberate tampering, or simple age can neutralize an ofuda without ever touching its maker. Stronger purposes cost proportionately greater mana, preparation, and paper of proper quality; inscribing beyond one's standing produces either a dead charm or a dangerously unstable one.",
