  "storyStarts": {
    "Random": {
      "name": "Random",
      "description": "Random arrival — the world decides how you got here and where you land. Let fate choose your starting point.",
      "storyStart": "[IF REINCARNATION: The player was born in Aerfála and has lived here their whole life. Write the opening as a native at a meaningful turning point — the day they decide to register at the Guild, leave their village, or take their first quest. Their cheat skill is an innate ability that manifested naturally. No arrival, no summoning, no portal.]\n\n[IF ANY OTHER ARRIVAL METHOD: The between-world has no name and no geometry. You are passing through it the way light passes through water — refracted, changed, and deposited somewhere the physics do not quite match what you remember. The transition ends. You arrive.]\n\nThe world has not yet learned your name. That changes today.",
      "locations": [],
      "locationAreas": [],
      "isDefault": false,
      "questGenerationGuidance": "Choose a random location and opening scenario based on the player's trait selections. CHECK the player's Arrival Method: if Reincarnation, the player is a native — write the opening as a local taking their first step into adventure, not an outsider arriving. If the player has a Companion trait (not None Solo Start), that companion MUST be present in the opening scene.",
      "startingItems": [],
      "startingPartyNPCs": [],
      "startingQuests": []
    },
    "A Debt Owed": {
      "description": "You arrived in Aerfála two weeks ago and incurred a significant debt to someone connected to the Merchant Houses. The creditor is patient, professional, and has made clear that patience has a timeline. CHECK Arrival Method: if Reincarnation, the player is a native who incurred the debt in-world, not a recent arrival.",
      "isDefault": false,
      "locationAreas": [
        "market-bridge"
      ],
      "locations": [
        "Riversplit"
      ],
      "name": "A Debt Owed",
      "questGenerationGuidance": "First quests focus on obtaining cp/sp/gp through actual Guild commissions, merchant work, sales, theft, negotiation, sponsorship, or another concrete means to pay or alter the debt. Introduce the Merchant Houses only through an actual contact, contract, location, report, or faction action. Romantic, sexual, comic, hostile, and strictly practical encounters follow the generated characters and circumstances rather than a required scene quota.",
      "startingItems": [],
      "startingPartyNPCs": [],
      "startingQuests": [],
      "storyStart": "You arrived in Aerfála two weeks ago. In that time you have managed to incur a significant debt to someone connected to the Merchant Houses — the details were confusing and the fine print was not in any language you knew at the time. The debt collector is patient, professional, and has made clear that patience has a timeline. You are in Riversplit, which is full of money moving in every direction, and you have exactly none of it. The creditor's representative is due to visit again tomorrow morning."
    },
    "Summoned Hero": {
      "description": "You were summoned to Aerfála by a royal ritual — pulled from your world into a throne room full of strangers who call you Hero. The kingdom has expectations. Whether you meet them is another matter entirely. NOTE: This start assumes a summoning arrival. If the player chose Reincarnation, the ritual identified them as the Hero — a native born with extraordinary power, not someone from another world. Adjust the opening accordingly.",
      "isDefault": false,
      "locationAreas": [
        "royal-quarter"
      ],
      "locations": [
        "Ríocht"
      ],
      "name": "Summoned Hero",
      "questGenerationGuidance": "First quests should focus on the royal summons, meeting the court, and being assigned your first mission. The Church and the Royal Knights both want the hero's allegiance. Introduce the political tensions between the court, the Church, and the Guild early. Include at least one romantic-appropriate encounter (noble's attention, formal ceremony, charged conversation) within the first three scenes. If the player has a Companion trait (not None Solo Start), that companion MUST be present in the opening scene — summoned alongside the hero, already at court, or manifesting in the throne room.",
      "startingItems": [],
      "startingPartyNPCs": [],
      "startingQuests": [],
      "storyStart": "Light explodes behind your eyes — not the soft glow of a screen, not the harsh flash of headlights, but something older and more deliberate. When your vision clears, you are standing in a circle of runes carved into marble, surrounded by people in robes and armor who are looking at you like you just saved their lives by existing.\n\n\"The Hero has answered our call,\" a woman in a white-and-gold vestment says, stepping forward. Her voice carries the practiced resonance of someone who gives sermons, not conversations. \"Welcome, Hero, to the Kingdom of Solasreach. The Demon Lord stirs beyond the frontier. We have prayed for your arrival. You are our hope.\"\n\nA man in ceremonial armor coughs pointedly. A younger woman in scholar's robes is staring at you with an expression that is either intense curiosity or intense skepticism. The king sits on a throne at the far end of the room, watching you with the measured patience of someone who has done this before and is not sure it worked.\n\n\"We have prepared lodging, provisions, and a Guild registration,\" the priestess continues. \"Your rank will be... assessed. The Guild does not recognize divine authority as a substitute for combat testing.\"\n\nSomewhere behind you, someone mutters something that sounds like \"another one.\" You are apparently not the first hero they have summoned.\n\nThe world has not yet learned your name. That changes today."
    },
    "Academy Arrival": {
      "description": "You have been enrolled at Crossroads Academy — the continent's premier magic and martial school, where nobility and scholarship students clash, demons hide in plain sight, and the curriculum is only half the education. CHECK Arrival Method: if Reincarnation, the player is a native student enrolling normally; if any other method, they arrived from another world and were enrolled upon arrival.",
      "isDefault": false,
      "locationAreas": [
        "courtyard"
      ],
      "locations": [
        "Crossroads Academy"
      ],
      "name": "Academy Arrival",
      "questGenerationGuidance": "First quests should focus on Academy orientation, class placement exams, and social navigation between noble and scholarship factions. Introduce at least one potential love interest and one rival within the first three scenes. Include at least one romantic-appropriate encounter within the first five scenes. The hidden demon operatives should be seeded as suspicious but unconfirmed. If the player has a Companion trait (not None Solo Start), that companion MUST be present in the opening scene. CHECK Arrival Method: if Reincarnation, the player is a native student — not an outsider from another world.",
      "startingItems": [],
      "startingPartyNPCs": [],
      "startingQuests": [],
      "storyStart": "The portal — or the summoning circle, or the truck, or whatever it was — deposits you not in a throne room or a field but on a flagstone courtyard surrounded by towers. A fountain burbles. Students in blue-and-silver uniforms glance at you and look away. A fox-kin woman with six tails and a clipboard is walking toward you with the particular stride of someone who has done this exact thing before.\n\n\"Another one,\" Professor Yuki Inari says, not unkindly. She checks something on her clipboard, makes a note, and looks you over with amber eyes that miss nothing. \"You're not from Aerfála. That's fine — we get three or four of you a year. Welcome to Crossroads Academy. You've been enrolled provisionally. Room and board covered. Tuition... we'll figure it out.\"\n\nShe hands you a packet — campus map, class schedule, dormitory assignment, and a single sheet titled \"Rules You Will Actually Follow\" that is exactly three items long:\n\n1. No killing other students. Duels are supervised or they do not happen.\n2. The restricted section of the library requires faculty authorization. Bypassing the ward counts as a supervised duel with the head librarian.\n3. What happens in the dormitories stays in the dormitories. We pretend we do not know. You pretend we do not know. Everyone is happier.\n\n\"The Headmistress will want to see you after orientation,\" Yuki adds, already turning to leave. \"Don't be late. She hates that.\"\n\nBehind you, the courtyard hums with the energy of a hundred conversations — nobles clustering in their self-appointed corners, scholarship students keeping their heads down and their ears open, and somewhere near the martial arena, the unmistakable sound of someone being thrown into sand. The gardens smell like jasmine and ambition.\n\nYou are not in Kansas anymore. You are not even sure Kansas exists here. But you have a dorm key, a class schedule, and a world that has not yet learned your name.\n\nThat changes today."
    },
    "Hero Gone Rogue": {
      "description": "You were summoned as a Hero and left. Now the crown knows your face and the Seekers of the Return have heard rumours. CHECK Arrival Method: if Reincarnation, the ritual identified the player as the Hero — a native who was called, not summoned from another world. They walked out anyway.",
      "isDefault": false,
      "locationAreas": [],
      "locations": [
        "Crossroads Inn"
      ],
      "name": "Hero Gone Rogue",
      "questGenerationGuidance": "First quests should navigate the tension between freedom and pursuit — the Royal Knights want you back, the Seekers of the Return are interested, and the Guild is uncertain. Include at least one romantic-appropriate encounter within the first three scenes. Create opportunities to choose between hiding and confronting your reputation.",
      "startingItems": [],
      "startingPartyNPCs": [],
      "startingQuests": [],
      "storyStart": "The royal summoning worked. You appeared in the throne room, the kingdom called you Hero, and then — you left. Maybe it was something they said. Maybe it was the weight of the obligation. Maybe you simply could not be what they needed and you knew it immediately. You have been travelling for three weeks since. The crown knows your face. The Seekers of the Return have heard rumours. The Adventurers' Guild does not know what to make of you. You are free in the specific way that only people being quietly pursued ever are."
    },
    "The Game Has Rules": {
      "description": "You played this world as a game. Now you are standing inside it and the NPCs are real people. CHECK Arrival Method: this start fits Sucked Into A Game best. If the player chose Reincarnation, adjust — they may have fragmentary memories of a game that matches this world, or meta-knowledge that feels like deja vu.",
      "isDefault": false,
      "locationAreas": [],
      "locations": [
        "Crossroads Inn"
      ],
      "name": "The Game Has Rules",
      "questGenerationGuidance": "First quests should explore the tension between meta-knowledge and moral responsibility — the player knows things but using that knowledge has consequences for real people. Include at least one romantic-appropriate encounter within the first three scenes. Let the player discover what has changed from the game they remember.",
      "startingItems": [],
      "startingPartyNPCs": [],
      "startingQuests": [],
      "storyStart": "You know this world. Not personally — you played it. The game was called something you cannot quite remember now that you are standing inside it, but you remember the mechanics. The faction reputation system, the romance routes, the dungeon layouts, the boss weaknesses. What you did not expect is how much the knowledge costs you — because everyone around you is real, and the NPCs have interiority, and the things that were plot points are people's lives. You are in the Crossroads Inn, which you remember from the tutorial zone, and you are reconsidering every advantage your knowledge gives you."
    },
    "Arrival in Aerfála": {
      "description": "You arrived from another world into the Anime Fantasy realm of Aerfála. Whether by reincarnation, transmigration, summoning, gate, or game-pull — you are here now, with a cheat skill, a class, and a story no one has written yet. Start in Ríocht, the continent's capital, where the Adventurers' Guild headquarters awaits your registration.",
      "isDefault": true,
      "locationAreas": [
        "guild-hall"
      ],
      "locations": [
        "Ríocht"
      ],
      "name": "Arrival in Aerfála",
      "questGenerationGuidance": "First quests should focus on Guild registration, orientation, and initial D-rank commissions. Introduce at least one potential companion or rival at the Guild. Include at least one romantic-appropriate encounter within the first three scenes. If the player has a Companion trait (not None Solo Start), that companion MUST be present in the opening scene. CHECK the player's Arrival Method: if Reincarnation, the player is a native — write them as a local registering at the Guild, not an outsider.",
      "startingItems": [],
      "startingPartyNPCs": [],
      "startingQuests": [],
      "storyStart": "[IF REINCARNATION: The player grew up in Aerfála. They have always been here. Their cheat skill is innate — it manifested during childhood, not received at arrival. Write the opening as a native walking into the Guild for the first time to register as an adventurer. The receptionist treats them as a local. No \"not from here\" or \"arrival\" language.]\n\n[IF ANY OTHER ARRIVAL METHOD: The player just arrived from another world. Write the opening below.]\n\nYou were someone else, somewhere else, doing something utterly ordinary — and then you were not.\n\nThe world changed around you like a page turning. One moment you were living a life you knew; the next, you were standing in a place that should not exist, breathing air that tastes like magic and feels like a promise. The sky above you is too blue. The stones beneath you are too clean. The woman at the desk in front of you is looking up from her paperwork with an expression that says she has seen this exact thing before and is already annoyed about the forms.\n\n\"Welcome to the Adventurers' Guild, Ríocht branch,\" she says, pushing a registration sheet across the counter. Her amber eyes flick over you once — assessing, cataloguing, and concluding something you cannot read. \"You're not from here. That's fine. Fill this out. Rank starts at F. Don't argue; everyone starts at F.\"\n\nThe sheet asks for: Name. Class. Any unusual skills or abilities you would like to declare.\n\nBehind you, the Guild Hall buzzes with the noise of a hundred conversations, the rustle of quest boards being scanned, and the particular energy of people who make their living by doing things that might kill them. Somewhere in the back, someone laughs too loud. Somewhere else, a sword rings against its scabbard.\n\nThe world has not yet learned your name. That changes today.\n\n[IF DEMI-PLANE — INNATE: You have a pocket dimension — a space that exists because you do. The Chamberlain is there now, waiting. You can access it with Demi-Plane Gate. The Anchor is in your inventory. The Demi-Plane is empty but full of potential.]"
    },
    "Dungeon, First Floor": {
      "description": "You woke up inside Shadowfen Dungeon with no memory of how you got there. Pure combat and exploration start.",
      "isDefault": false,
      "locationAreas": [],
      "locations": [
        "Shadowfen Dungeon"
      ],
      "name": "Dungeon, First Floor",
      "questGenerationGuidance": "First quests should focus on survival, exploration, and finding an exit. No faction connection, no social obligations. Let the player establish character through action before worldbuilding. Include at least one encounter with a significant creature or discovery within the first three scenes.",
      "startingItems": [],
      "startingPartyNPCs": [],
      "startingQuests": [],
      "storyStart": "You woke up here. Not outside the entrance — inside it. First floor, stone floor, the sound of something moving in the dark ahead. Your class abilities are active. Your cheat skill is available. You have no memory of how you arrived at this specific location and a pressing reason not to think about it right now. Somewhere ahead is an exit. Somewhere behind you, the entrance presumably exists. The choice of direction is yours."
    },
    "Demon Prince-Princess": {
      "description": "A soul poured into a demon's ancestral egg, hatching as a true-blooded Demon Prince-Princess with a fief to claim and a fractious court to manage. This is a Reincarnation start — you were born in Aerfála, not summoned from another world.",
