    {
      "attributes": {},
      "backstory": "You were beta-testing a VRMMO called Aerfála Online — your studio's flagship\nproject, three years in development, scheduled to launch in six months. You were\nthe lead systems designer. You knew every variable in the game.\n\nYou logged in for a routine QA pass and could not log out. The disconnect button\ndid nothing. The menu would not open. Your character's HP bar was real. So was\nthe pain when you stubbed your toe on the floor of the inn you had spawned in.\n\nThat was four months ago. Your familiar is the slime you adopted in week two, who\nhas named itself Pip and is approximately the size of a watermelon. Your cheat\nskill is the developer's interface — you can spawn items from the asset library,\nread source code on objects you focus on, and access systems that the actual\nresidents of Aerfála treat as physical laws. You have not yet figured out whether\nthey are real people or unusually sophisticated NPCs. You have started to suspect\nit does not matter.\n\nYour memories from before the game are foggy in places that worry you — you\nremember designing the game, but you cannot quite remember your sister's face.\nYou wonder sometimes whether you wrote yourself into the game on purpose. You\nwonder whether you should be trying harder to get out.\n\nMostly you are having fun. The food is good. The friends are real. The magic\nsystem you designed turns out to be deeply satisfying to use from the inside.\nAnd there is a fox-kin professor at the Academy who keeps asking you very pointed\nquestions about your knowledge of the world's underlying mechanics, and you are\nnot sure whether you should tell her the truth.\n",
      "description": "A twenty-two-year-old game developer who got sucked into the VRMMO she was beta-testing — and then realized halfway through that the game was a real world. Her cheat skill is the developer's interface she designed for QA testing. She can spawn items. She can read source code. She has not yet decided whether to abuse this.",
