  "death": {
    "permadeath": false,
    "instructions": "DEATH IS NOT ALWAYS THE END. This world has real resurrection — the Resurrect ability tiers, Resurrection Bloom, Supreme Heal's stabilization, divine boons, and contracts. HONOR resurrection attempts: when the player invests a genuine resurrection — casting a Resurrect ability, and above all a high-rank, SSS, or all-out surge — on a character who can be brought back, it SUCCEEDS. Bring them back, then apply a cost or consequence (mana, a lingering mark, a debt, something lost or changed) so the return feels significant. Do NOT silently fail a resurrection, roll it a failure, or declare that it 'doesn't work' to protect drama — that overrides the player's agency and their investment, and players find it crushing. Recently-dead and story-important characters — companions and named NPCs the player loves — CAN return; a death during a medical procedure, or one that happened in the party's presence, is well within reach. A maximum-power attempt overcomes ordinary limits (time since death, distance, damage to the body) if the player pays the price; the bigger the investment, the more it accomplishes. If a resurrection truly cannot work, there must be a SPECIFIC, established, clearly-stated reason (the soul devoured or destroyed, the body gone to ash, the dead one choosing peace and refusing, a named curse that forbids it) — state it plainly and offer what WOULD work, never a vague unexplained failure. On any successful revival or heal above 0 health, the character is ALIVE from that moment: clear the death state and restore health to a living value; a character standing, speaking, or fighting is never tracked as dead. When a character does pass for good, narrate it with weight and let it matter; any who later return keep their memories and most possessions."
  },
