      "storyMessage": "Narrator: <focus:Hakon> The morning wears on. Hakon makes his slow rounds of the square's edge, boat-hook across his shoulders, pausing at the alley mouth each pass to peer down the shadowed gap between the rows.\n\nHakon: [gruffly, low] Still quiet. Too quiet for my liking, but quiet's what we've got.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Corvath Salt> Corvath's whispering grows audible — a cracked, endless thread of pleading, names of gods he's never worshipped tumbling over each other. His trembling has not stopped since dawn; his knuckles are white where he grips his own arms.\n\nCorvath Salt: [whispering, frantic] Please... please, I gave them everything, I kept the ledgers true for twenty years, I— please, anyone...\n\nNarrator: <focus:foxfire paper sentinel 2> The Closed Hand's sentinel turns its blank fox mask toward him at the sound. The whispering stops at once.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Hakon> Across the square, the Brine-Soaked Barrel's doors open. Council members file out — a fishwife in a good shawl, a quay-master, an old priest of the tide-altar — and the crowd that has been loitering since dawn surges toward the square's edges.\n\nHakon: [loudly, gruff] Council's come! Back and give them room — you've waited all morning, you can wait ten more steps back!\n\nNarrator: <focus:Tsukiko Kuzunoha> The council takes its places before the execution block — three chairs brought from the Barrel, set facing the shrine you built. The old priest looks at the lanterns, the incense, the four lantern-names laid to the winds, and inclines his head slowly toward you.\n\nPriest of the Tide-Altar: [gravely, quiet] Whoever laid this rite did it properly. The dead have their morning. Now the living will have their judgment. Bring the prisoner forward.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Hakon> Hakon hooks a hand under Corvath's arm and hauls him upright. The man's legs buckle twice before they hold him; the sentinel glides at his back the whole way to the block.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Corvath Salt> Corvath stands before the council, swaying, salt-bleached robes hanging off him like they belong to a larger man. His eyes dart from face to face, finding no mercy anywhere.\n\nCorvath Salt: [shaking, hoarse] I... I have confessed everything. I'll sign anything. I'll— the records, the gold, all of it, it's yours, just... just say what must be said and be done with me.\n\nNarrator: The fishwife councilor opens the marked ledgers Elias Thorne delivered, and the square falls silent as the tide going out.",
