      "storyMessage": "Narrator: <focus:June Hallow> The fishwife councilor's finger stops on a page. She reads without hurry, voice carrying flat and clear across the square.\n\nJune Hallow: [coldly, clearly] \"Winter tithe, ten years past. Forty gold collected from the pans, recorded as forty gold delivered. The pans paid sixty.\" Twenty gold, vanished into one man's purse — while miners ate weed-soup that winter. Do you remember, Salt? The ledgers do.\n\nNarrator: <focus:June Hallow> She looks up from the page at him, and there is nothing in her face but hardened disgust.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Torin Reef> The old priest closes his eyes, thumb moving slow across his knuckles. When he speaks, his voice is low and resonant, like water over stone.\n\nTorin Reef: [lowly, gravely] Corvath Salt. Before this council rules, answer one thing. Do you seek the mercy of the tides, or the judgment of the law? Know that the spirits of Olem, of Vass, of Bren Mill stand watching you even now. The tides may wash a man clean. They do not wash away what he fed to them.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Corvath Salt> Corvath's legs give out. He drops to his knees on the flagstones and crawls a half-step, clutching at the hem of the fishwife's shawl with both trembling hands.\n\nCorvath Salt: [sobbing, desperate] Mercy — please, I was afraid, that's all it ever was, I was afraid — ten years, I've carried it ten years — reduce it, any sentence but the block, I'll wear chains, I'll dig salt till I die, please, lady, please—\n\nNarrator: <focus:Hakon> Hakon crosses the space in three strides, seizes Corvath by the collar, and hauls him off her like a dog off a table leg. He slams him back against the execution block, forearm across the man's chest, pinning him there.\n\nHakon: [angry, gruff] She's a councilor, not your mother. Kneel where you're put and answer the priest. You've begged enough for one lifetime — the dead haven't heard a single honest word yet.",
