      "storyMessage": "Narrator: <focus:Torin Reef> Torin Reef opens his eyes at last. He looks down at the kneeling man for a long, tide-slow moment, and there is no triumph in his face — only a profound, heavy sadness.\n\nTorin Reef: [gravely, low] The law cries out for blood, and it would be owed. But the tides demand truth, not death — death ends a man's paying. So hear the ruling of this council, Corvath Salt. You are sentenced to lifelong servitude — to the families you robbed, to the widows and orphans whose bread you stole, and to the spirits you betrayed. Every coin, every hour, every year of what remains to you belongs to them. You will dig their salt, carry their water, and kneel at their doors until the tides take you. The block is denied you. That is not mercy. That is the weight.\n\nNarrator: <focus:June Hallow> June Hallow rises from her chair and steps forward, ledger tucked under one arm, voice ringing flat and clear across the square.\n\nJune Hallow: [coldly, clearly] And the council fixes the terms. The gold recovered from the cellar — six thousand one hundred, counted before witnesses — becomes a permanent trust for the orphans and widows of his victims, held by the town under council seal. His house, his holdings, his remaining assets — seized to fund it. He stole from the living to feed his fear. He will spend his life paying it back.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Hakon> Hakon's grip tightens on Corvath's collar until the man chokes. He leans close, and his voice is a low, cutting sneer.\n\nHakon: [coldly, gruff] You begged for mercy, Salt. There it is. A whole lifetime of it — paying back every single coin, to every single face you'd rather never see again. Look at them. They'll be looking at you every morning for the rest of your life.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Corvath Salt> Corvath's face goes slack, then crumples. The strangled wail that comes out of him is not rage — it is the sound of a man watching his whole life dissolve at once: the office, the robes,