      "storyMessage": "Narrator: <focus:Tsukiko Kuzunoha> You pick one warm-gold fruit from the vine and bite into it, juice bright and clean on your tongue — sweet like ripe pear, with a low, humming warmth that spreads through your chest and settles there, steady as banked embers.\n\nTsukiko Kuzunoha: [softly, warm] Mm. It restores mana — gently, not greedily. A gardener's fruit, not a warrior's. [lightly] And you were right, dear. It is remarkable.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Chamberlain> The Chamberlain watches you eat the uncatalogued specimen with the expression of a scholar watching a colleague lick a page to test the ink, then opens the ledger and writes the ruling — a precise, ink-heavy stroke beneath the day's entries.\n\nChamberlain: [calmly, dry] Recorded. Traders chosen by merit, entry earned, never granted — and each inhabitant governs their own commerce. Benevolence noted, Creator. Though I will observe, without opinion, that benevolence without central direction often produces... unfortunate inconsistencies. One trader sells your fruit honestly. Another dilutes it. A third sells the recipe to a rival. Quality drifts, and the name suffers before it is ever established.\n\nChamberlain: [lightly, measured] So — a compromise, if you will permit one. Not command. Guidelines. A short code of conduct for any who choose to trade outward: honest measure, no sale of the realm's secrets, disputes brought home to be settled here. They may follow it or refuse it. But the estate keeps its standards on paper, so no one can claim they never existed.\n\nChamberlain: [dryly, expectant] Shall I draft it? Or would you prefer your marketplace discover its own chaos first, and write the rules in bruises?",
