      "storyMessage": "Narrator: <focus:Tsukiko Kuzunoha> You take the draft page from him and read it aloud, voice low and even.\n\nTsukiko Kuzunoha: Clause One: Honest Measure — all goods leaving the realm weighed and priced transparently, no hidden fees, no deceptive scales; a cheating merchant is banned for one lunar cycle. Inspection protocol: declared versus carried, prohibited items confiscated on sight, provenance honestly explained or the goods do not pass. Contraband logged — item, quantity, name of the carrier. No exceptions, kin included. [warmly] It is precise and fair.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Chamberlain> The Chamberlain inclines his head, opens the ledger, and flips back to the logistics section with two fingers.\n\nChamberlain: [calmly, dry] Then a practical question follows, Creator: where does confiscated contraband live between seizure and judgment? My proposal — a secure, magically sealed vault near the Gateway Clearing, inside the inspection lane itself. Nothing enters or leaves it but by your word. And for its guard, your foxfire paper sentinels. They do not sleep, do not bargain, and do not grow fond of prisoners. The evidence remains untouched until you pass fox judgment.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Tsukiko Kuzunoha> You consult the ledger with him, tracing the column of entries with one fingertip as he marks the vault's proposed site against the Gateway Clearing's ground-plan.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Caspian Vane> A dry cough announces a third presence. A thin man in charcoal-grey robes, silver hair pulled into a tight ponytail, steps up to the development board — a heavy wooden inkwell strapped to his belt like a sidearm.\n\nCaspian Vane: [stiffly formal] Forgive the intrusion. Caspian Vane — formerly of the royal archives, currently of your ledger's shadow. I have read the draft over the Chamberlain's shoulder, and it is grammatically sound, which is rarer than you would hope. But I raise one objection to his vault scheme.\n\nChamberlain: [dryly] You object to something. Go on.\n\nCaspian Vane: [precisely] A single record is a single point of failure. Fire, damp, tampering, one careless sentinel with a lantern — and the proof of a smuggler's crime becomes an unprovable rumor. I propose a duplicate archive, kept separately, maintained by my own hand. My copies are exact. Perfectly exact. If the vault record and my record ever disagree, one of them has been touched by someone who should not have touched it — and that disagreement is itself evidence.\n\nChamberlain: [measured, faintly grudging] ...That is, irritatingly, correct. Redundancy in records, singularity in judgment. The vault holds the goods; Vane's archive holds the truth of what was seized. Both under your authority alone, Creator.\n\nCaspian Vane: [stiffly] I would also enter, while I have the floor, the estate's standing report — first full assessment, as none has been taken. I have drafted it. Miasma: stable. The garden's root-networks breathe clean; no corruption pools anywhere in the Basin. Military: adequate — two foxfire paper sentinels on Chamberlain's duty, the inspection lane unbuilt, and otherwise a realm whose chief weapon is its founder. Adequate is the honest word.\n\nChamberlain: [calmly] Prestige: contested. The outside world does not know this realm exists — which means no one honors it and no one fears it. That is a door we have not yet opened, and the code in your hand is the first thing we would be judged by when we do.\n\nCaspian Vane: [dryly critical] And subjects: skeleton staff. Ume, four attendants, two sentinels, one archivist, one Chamberlain. Nine souls to hold a realm. Every dwelling on this row but ours stands clean and unclaimed. [firmly] The single problem I would put before you today, Creator, is that one — a code of conduct and an empty border mean nothing while there is no one here to trade, to judge, or to be judged. The realm needs residents before it needs merchants.\n\nChamberlain: [lightly, expectant] He is not wrong,