      "storyMessage": "Tsukiko Kuzunoha: [warmly, softly] Thank you, Taka. Your work today saved more lives than you know. I could not have asked for better.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Taka> The little paper fox chirps, nuzzling its porcelain mask against your palm, its whole body vibrating with pride.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Genkurō> You turn to Genkurō and Hoshi-maru, still standing rigid along the ramparts.\n\nTsukiko Kuzunoha: [gently, formal] Genkurō, Hoshi-maru — your wall held against everything they had. I am proud of you both. Rest your forms while you can.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Genkurō> Both constructs bow in perfect, synchronized precision, parchment crackling softly as they straighten back to their posts.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Tsukiko Kuzunoha> You turn to the runner shikigami hovering patiently at your side.\n\nTsukiko Kuzunoha: [softly] And you — you kept your post faithfully through all of it. Well done. Stay close a while longer.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Hadrian Pike> Pike huffs a short, rattling laugh and rubs the back of his neck with a bloodied gauntlet, watching the exchange with something like wonder.\n\nGarrison Sergeant: [gruff, dry] Saint or stowaway, priestess — I don't much care which. You keep my men breathing, that's all I need to know about you.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Hadrian Pike> He jerks his chin toward the ridgeline, where the haze is already thickening again at the edges.\n\nGarrison Sergeant: [gruff, pragmatic] But here's my worry. They pulled back organized — not routed. That horn went silent, but I heard a second one earlier, from the far side. They're regrouping somewhere I can't see. Can you spare any more of that light of yours to help us secure the perimeter? Eyes on the slopes, the ford approach, the scrub — before they test us again.",
