        "Tsukiko Kuzunoha": "I look at the soldiers \"If one of you threatens my guest again lr forbids him my hospitality then you have larger worries than the demons. Do your duties but do not think you have any command over me or my doings.\" Then i look at the scout \"Drink. Would you also like something to eat? I do not have much but an empty stomach is rarely picky.\""
      },
      "storyMessage": "Tsukiko Kuzunoha: [calmly, coolly] If one of you threatens my guest again, he loses my hospitality — and then you have larger worries than the demons. Do your duties. But do not think you have any command over me or my doings.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Hadrian Pike> Pike holds your gaze for a breath. Then he steps back a few inches — not far, and his sword stays drawn — and jerks his chin at Gant.\n\nHadrian Pike: [gruff, flat] Get your parchment. Record every detail about the garden province and the House Vael marks. If he so much as flinches toward a weapon, put him down.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Elias Gant> Gant lowers the crossbow from the scout's chest to a low ready, safety still off, and pulls a stained notebook and charcoal pencil from his kit. He steps closer, grim and expectant.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Tsukiko Kuzunoha> You turn to the scout and lift the second cup toward him.\n\nTsukiko Kuzunoha: [softly, warm] Drink. Would you also like something to eat? I do not have much, but an empty stomach is rarely picky.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Gray-Walker Scout> The scout looks at the cup, then at you, and something in those mismatched eyes softens. His hand trembles as he takes the tea.\n\nGray-Walker Scout: [raspy, quiet] Do you have any bread? Or dried fruit? I have not eaten since the last moon.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Tsukiko Kuzunoha> You reach into thin air and draw out a wrapped travel ration — hard bread, a strip of dried fruit — and set it on the lacquer tray beside him.\n\nTsukiko Kuzunoha: [gently] Here. Eat slowly. Your stomach will thank you for the patience.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Gray-Walker Scout> The scout breaks the bread with both hands and eats in small, careful bites, steam from the cup fogging his grey chin. He keeps his eyes on you rather than the soldiers, as if the tea and food are the only solid things on this bank.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Elias Gant> Gant licks the charcoal pencil and waits, notebook open, voice flat as slate.\n\nElias Gant: [grim, low] Start again, half-blood. The stones — where exactly, and what mark did you see. Slow, so I can write it.",
