      "playerInputs": {
        "Tsukiko Kuzunoha": "I sot with him and sip on my tea \"Ignore them for now. You're my guest and not theirs to interrogate. But if you would not mind telling me - you say that humans started this and wanted the soldiers here so another house could steal land? Or what exactly are you saying?\" Then i carch myself, apologize formally for my lack of manners and introduce myself humbly before any talk continues."
      },
      "storyMessage": "Narrator: <focus:Tsukiko Kuzunoha> You settle onto the flat stone beside him, knees folding into a perfect seiza, and sip your tea while Gant's pencil scratches. Then you catch yourself — a small, rueful breath.\n\nTsukiko Kuzunoha: [softly, formally] Forgive my rudeness. I offered bread before I offered a name — a host's failing. I am Tsukiko Kuzunoha, a wanderer of no banner and no house. It is my honor to receive you at this grey water.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Gray-Walker Scout> The scout sets down his empty cup. Something in his bearing changes — shoulders lowering, hands resting open on his knees, the trembling gone.\n\nGray-Walker Scout: [hoarse, quieter] Talin. Of the Ashen Steppe kinships. You are the first to give a name before a question, priestess Tsukiko. I will answer yours.\n\nGray-Walker Scout: [hoarse, low] Not humans — not your kind alone. House Vael is demon nobility. They want the soldiers here — your garrison, their patrols — bleeding each other over the ford. While the spears are busy, the stones move. Each stone they shift drags the miasma-line east with it. Where their mark falls, the trade road dies, and the ground becomes theirs by old right.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Elias Gant> Gant's pencil stops mid-stroke. His eyes lift from the notebook to Pike, the tic in his cheek jumping hard.\n\nElias Gant: [grim, flat] Sergeant. If that's true, the road through the steppe chokes inside a season — and every caravan toll between here and the coast goes into Vael's vault.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Hadrian Pike> Pike says nothing at first. He tightens the leather strap on his shield — once, twice — then gives you a short, respectful nod.\n\nHadrian Pike: [gruff, low] Tsukiko Kuzunoha. A name I'll remember. Just keep your guests from getting too comfortable — we're still in a war zone.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Talin> Talin looks at Pike, then back to you, and speaks evenly, as if reciting a lesson learned in blood.\n\nTalin: [hoarse, steady] The markers are the old garden stones — carved with a vine-and-sun sign no one living has honored. The first lies a half-day east, by the dry orchard rows. They moved it with chains and six oxen, in silence, on the dark of the moon. The second sits at the split-rock ford upstream. The third I did not see — I only heard the wagons rolling north before dawn. And the men who do the moving burn everything they pass. My cousin was ash by the time we found what remained.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Elias Gant> Gant writes until the pencil's edge crumbles, then taps the page flat with two fingers.\n\nElias Gant: [grim, quiet] Three stones, three bearings, a house-mark, and a body count. That's enough to hang a noble house if it survives the trip up the chain.",
