    {
      "type": "complex",
      "tick": 171,
      "playerInputs": {
        "Tsukiko Kuzunoha": "I speak sincere condolences and a prayer for his cousin so his soul may find peace or the kindness of the yellow springs to grant him to cross the bridge into a new life before i ask \"And why would anyone be moving the stones? It sounds important yet i have no knowledge about why. Is there a meaning to it?\""
      },
      "storyMessage": "Narrator: <focus:Tsukiko Kuzunoha> You set down your cup, fold your hands in your lap, and bow your head slightly toward Talin.\n\nTsukiko Kuzunoha: [softly, sincere] Then let us speak of him first, before stones and houses. May your cousin's soul find peace — may the yellow springs receive him kindly, and the bridge carry him across into a new life, unburdened by ash. I will offer a prayer for him tonight, with proper rites, if you will permit it.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Talin> Talin stares at you for a long moment. His throat works once, twice, and the mist in his mismatched eyes is not entirely the river haze.\n\nTalin: [hoarse, quiet] ...No one has prayed for him. Not once. The kinships found only ash and boot-prints. You honor him, priestess. I permit it — gladly.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Talin> He sets the empty cup down and turns toward the water, voice steadier now, reciting what he knows like a man handing over something fragile.\n\nTalin: [hoarse, low] You asked why the stones are moved. It is not theft, priestess. The boundary stones are anchors — each one pins the land's spirit to its place. Where a stone stands, the ground remembers what it was. Move the stone, and the memory is rewritten. The miasma follows the new line like a dog on a chain. House Vael is not stealing land. They are rewriting the laws of the ground itself — so that only those who command the miasma can live on it.\n\nTalin: [hoarse, bitter] The old garden province was green once. My kinships walked it before the first Demon Lord poisoned the soil. The stones were set when it was still a garden. Move them east, and the garden's spirit suffocates — and everything on the trade road with it.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Hadrian Pike> Pike lets out a sharp huff through his nose and steps closer, boots grinding on the shingle, eyes sweeping the scrub line on the far bank.\n\nHadrian Pike: [gruff, cynical] The meaning's simple, priestess. Power and coin. Vael shifts the line, the road dies, the tolls go into their vault, and anyone who can't breathe miasma clears off or dies. That's the whole arithmetic.\n\nHadrian Pike: [low, grim] And understand this — if they're playing this game, the men moving those stones won't stop at three. They'll keep going until every witness on the steppe is ash and the map is redrawn in their favor. Your scout's cousin won't be the last body in the scrub.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Elias Gant> Gant snaps his notebook shut and looks from Pike to Talin, jaw tight.\n\nElias Gant: [grim, flat] Sergeant — if the stones anchor the land's spirit, then moving them back isn't just surveying. It's putting the ground right again. That's beyond anything a garrison can do with spears.\n\nHadrian Pike: [gruff, dry] Beyond spears, aye. But not, I'm thinking, beyond a priestess who kills demons with foxfire and pours tea while the line holds.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Talin> Talin turns from the water and looks at you — a long, measuring look, the first real hope you've seen in his mismatched eyes.\n\nTalin: [hoarse, hesitant] The stones can be set back. The old rites know how — the kinships remember fragments, songs from before the poisoning. But it would need power, priestess. Power the steppe has not seen since the garden died.",
