        "Tsukiko Kuzunoha": "I do accept their conditions. They are fair. After all i'd want to know i'm not being fooled as well. Also, i extend my invite to Talin and his kin he was going to warn. If they are willing to be bound then i am willing to give them a home and work."
      },
      "storyMessage": "Tsukiko Kuzunoha: [softly, sincere] I accept your conditions — they are fair. I would want to know I was not being fooled, in your place. And Talin — my offer is yours as well. When you go to warn your kin, tell them this: if they are willing to be bound, I am willing to give them a home and work. All of them.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Talin> Talin goes very still. His mismatched eyes widen, and his long hands drop slowly to his sides.\n\nTalin: [hoarse, unsteady] A home. For the shadow-walkers. Priestess, my kin have not had those words spoken for them in three generations. I will carry them true — every camp, every hiding-hole, I will speak your offer exactly as you gave it.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Ronan> Ronan crouches, pressing his palm flat to the sulfurous soil, reading the vibration and the wind's drift. He rises and points a long ashen finger toward jagged basalt pillars to the east.\n\nRonan: [rasping, certain] The knight's tracks run there — toward the Low-Silt Wash. Half a day's march to the orchard road, if we keep to the blind-spots. The crust is thin between those pillars. Step where I step, or the ground will cook what's inside your boots.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Syril> Syril laughs his dry rattle and adjusts the strap of his scrap-metal gear.\n\nSyril: [rasping, wry] Since I'm bound by riddles now, I'll keep my nose in the wind for Vael scouts in the vents. If the air starts smelling of burnt ozone, that's my signal — you'll hear me hiss twice. After that, run or fight, but don't stand in the open admiring the view.\n\nHadrian Pike: [low, commanding] Good. You three take point — staggered line, twenty paces ahead. You flush ambushes before my soldiers eat them. Pace steady, noise low. We move now.\n\nElias Gant: [grim, muttering] Wonderful. Hope the riddle-craft has a clause about not marching us straight into a Vael killing field.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Hadrian Pike> Pike gives a sharp single nod, and the soldiers rise from the scrub in silence, spears leveled east toward the pillars.",
