      "storyMessage": "Narrator: <focus:Tsukiko Kuzunoha> You give Taka's porcelain mask a grateful stroke, and the little fox trills a papery chirp before folding tight against your shoulder.\n\nTsukiko Kuzunoha: [softly, warm] Thank you, dear. Well done.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Tsukiko Kuzunoha> You turn to the crouched soldiers and trace the same shape in the air as you speak — a broad ring, a column rising from its heart, small figures ringing the rim.\n\nTsukiko Kuzunoha: [low, clear] A circle of stones or stakes, wide enough for a hundred bodies, with a pillar of light at its center — not fire, something fed. The marks around the edge are watchers, spaced like sentries. Taka counted at least a dozen standing posts. This is not a camp, Sergeant. It is a gathering, held in order.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Hadrian Pike> Pike stays in his crouch, shield braced against the crust, eyes fixed on the basin's rim where the white glow bleeds upward into the haze. His jaw works once.\n\nHadrian Pike: [low, gruff] A gathering. Demons don't hold ceremonies in open ground unless they mean something by it. Gant — you ever seen anything like that in three campaigns?\n\nElias Gant: [grim, muttering] Once. Border of the old garden province, years back. A noble house held a court under open sky — banners, a light-pyre, the whole performance. They were demonstrating something for the Demon Lord's inspectors. Anyone who wandered in uninvited didn't wander out.\n\nTiran Gale: [whispering, tight] So we go around. Right, Sergeant? We don't need whatever that is.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Hadrian Pike> Pike doesn't answer Gale immediately. His thumb runs along the rim of his shield, and his gaze slides from the wash to you and back.\n\nHadrian Pike: [low, flat] Orchards road runs along that basin's far lip. Around means adding hours over thin crust in the dark — or through it, quiet, while they're busy with their show. Priestess. Your paper-fox says watchers. Does it say what kind of demons are down 