      "playerInputs": {
        "Tsukiko Kuzunoha": "I look at dorian \"A court bending its own rules? So you are openly undermining your own rules and the empress then? If one can bend your rules, dorian, tell me why they should abide by them then if they are this.. loose.\" Then i look at the empress \"Boring? No. An orderly court is not boring. It is what keeps you sitting where you are. As to your question - i have my own system in a way. Details are not needed for now since i do not trust anyone in this room to keep them instead of spreading them. Yet, what i can tell you is that in my life, which has been long, i have never had my own disrespect me publicly like dorian did with you.\""
      },
      "storyMessage": "Narrator: <focus:Dorian Vael> Dorian Vael holds your gaze for a long beat. Then he gives a slow, singular nod — the nod of an officer conceding ground he cannot defend.\n\nDorian Vael: [measured, dry] A fair point. Many would mistake a lack of trust for wisdom, but in this court, it is the only currency that holds value. You speak of respect and public dignity — concepts often discarded when the blade is at the throat. I will not pretend a bent rule is an unbent one. I will say only that I have bent it deliberately, and I will answer for that to the Empress, not to you.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Lilessa the Ashen Empress> Above, the Empress laughs — low and delighted, the sound rolling down through the gallery like spilled wine. She leans further over the perch rail, eyes glittering.\n\nLilessa the Ashen Empress: [languid, pleased] Such a sharp tongue for such a soft build. You claim you do not trust us with your secrets, yet you stand here and dissect my officer's failures with the precision of a surgeon. I find this... intoxicating. Tell me, little lantern — if I were to offer you a place where your 'system' could be tested against the finest minds of the abyss, would you still cling to your silence?\n\nNarrator: <focus:Tiran Gale> Gale makes a small, stifled whimper. His spear-tip scratches the floor as he shifts his weight, casting a terrified glance between the perch and your back.\n\nNarrator: <focus:adjudicator 1> The pale adjudicator makes another sharp mark on his tally-stave, four pupils tracking you with mechanical rhythm. He says nothing.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Dorian Vael> Dorian's thumb stills against his hilt. When he speaks again, his voice has lost its glacial edge — replaced by something quieter, more careful.\n\nDorian Vael: [dry, formal] Be mindful how you answer that, guest. An invitation from the Empress is rarely only an invitation.",
