      "storyMessage": "Narrator: <focus:Lilessa the Ashen Empress> Lilessa drapes herself onto a bone-carved seat at the platform's edge, silk pooling, and waves a hand at the swirling vortex below as though presenting a dinner table.\n\nLilessa the Ashen Empress: [languid, amused] Tea is for guests who linger, little lantern. You asked for the test — so here it is. No pleasantries. Begin the first weight, Verifier.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Vassago the Verifier> Vassago steps forward, needle fingers hovering over his ledger, all four amber eyes fixed on you.\n\nVassago the Verifier: [coldly, precise] The Empress's request is simple. You will withstand a series of ideological and systemic pressures — propositions, contradictions, temptations — without your foundation buckling. I will be the one to measure the fractures. Answer or refuse as you like; both are recorded.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Kaelen the Hollow> The old man's laugh is a thin rattle, like paper in wind. He tilts his clouded eyes toward you.\n\nKaelen the Hollow: [dry, weary] Tell me, mortal — do you believe your silence is a shield? Or merely a void, waiting to be filled by the abyss?\n\nTsukiko Kuzunoha: [calmly, sighing] I see all your subordinates are as talkative as Dorian. Am I here for a conversation over tea, or can we simply do whatever test was intended?\n\nNarrator: <focus:Vassago the Verifier> Vassago makes a single inked notation, unhurried, then lifts the ledger toward you like a blade being drawn.\n\nVassago the Verifier: [coldly] First weight, then. You claim your foundations are unshakable. So: your companions — the two soldiers you bartered for — are guests of this court on your word alone. If you fail a weight, the court may claim one of them as lawful compensation. Do you accept terms where your certainty has a price, or do you reveal now that your system protects only yourself?",
