      "storyMessage": "Narrator: <focus:Zeryth> Zeryth's surface ripples, spikes leaning toward your palm, and it pulses once — a silent bow of acknowledgment — before dissolving back into a still, observant pool at the platform's edge, its attention fixed on the Verifier.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Dorian Vael> Dorian gives a sharp, curt bow of the head, precise as a blade salute.\n\nDorian Vael: [low, dry] Your temper is noted, and your lack of patience is a loud signal. Since you find my presence tedious, I shall step back and let the same silence you crave be the only thing that accompanies your failures.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Dorian Vael> He withdraws to the far edge of the platform, rigid as a pike, and says nothing more.\n\nKaelen the Hollow: [dry, rattling] Do not confuse my flaws with your answers. You gave the requested demonstration, yet you seek to lecture the lecturer. If you believe you have succeeded, then perhaps you are the one failing to understand the nature of a weight. The answer is not the point; the reaction is.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Vassago the Verifier> Vassago draws a final, definitive stroke across his ledger, ink glistening on the page.\n\nVassago the Verifier: [coldly, precise] The demonstration is recorded. The contradiction is absent, yet the arrogance is voluminous. The first weight is resolved as Stable — though the method was unorthodox. We move to the second weight immediately, as the guest's patience is evidently a finite resource.\n\nLilessa the Ashen Empress: [laughing, low and musical] Let her rage. A lantern that burns this hot is far more interesting than one that merely glows. Proceed, Vassago — let us see if her foundations are as unshakable as her tongue is sharp.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Vassago the Verifier> The Verifier turns a page. The vortex beneath the stage churns faster, the mana-siphon's hum climbing a note.\n\nVassago the Verifier: [coldly, precise] Second weight. You claim order without fear. Then consider: your order binds souls by intent — so you have told your Chamberlain. Intent is judged by the judge, not the judged. Proposition two: every judgment of intent is tyranny wearing kindness, because the judged never consented to the standard. State whether you agree — or demonstrate that you do not, and be measured by how.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Kaelen the Hollow> The old man's clouded eyes turn toward you with unsettling exactness, and his thin smile widens.\n\nKaelen the Hollow: [dry, weary] Ah. He has found the seam in your stitching, mortal. You judge who enters your sanctuary. Answer carefully — or don't. I collect both.",
