      "storyParagraphs": [
        "Narrator: <focus:Tsukiko Kuzunoha> You look up at the humming instrument, and the word that leaves you is not a request. Divine Authority moves through your voice like a blade through silk — the air itself stiffens, every demon on the platform feeling their spines bend toward you without their consent.",
        "Tsukiko Kuzunoha: [coldly, absolute] Siphon. Erase everything you have read of me. Refuse, and you will be unmade.",
        "Narrator: <focus:Vassago the Verifier> <hit:211:0> The instrument shudders — then obeys. A pulse of violet light floods down the siphon's throat and reverses, the vortex beneath it flushing backward, streams of stolen memory unwriting themselves into nothing. Vassago throws himself flat over his ledger, whispering hoarsely.",
        "Vassago the Verifier: [urgent, strained] Guest — wait — that siphon is the only objective record this court has ever kept. Every weighing, every verdict, every truth spoken on this stage for three centuries flows through it. Blind it and the court loses its memory — the honest and the guilty alike lose their proof. I beg you weigh what you erase!",
        "Narrator: <focus:Lilessa the Ashen Empress> Lilessa does not move to stop it. She watches the violet streams dissolve with her chin in her hand, molten eyes reflecting the reversal — and she laughs, low and slow.",
        "Lilessa the Ashen Empress: [teasing, soft] No. Don't stop her, Verifier. Let her choose — erase my pretty eye like a woman of real authority, or smash it in a tantrum like a mortal pretending. Either way, little lantern, you show me something worth keeping.",
        "Narrator: <focus:Dorian Vael> Dorian lies where he fell, breath shallow, arms and legs ruined beneath him. He does not reach for his blade. Only his eyes track the dying light of the siphon, clinical and patient, a broken soldier cataloguing exactly what a guest may do to a court and survive.",
        "Dorian Vael: [low, pained, dry] ...Noted for the record. The siphon submitted. The court's eye is closing. No protocol governs what happens next."
      ],
